time limit per test: 1 second
memory limit per test: 256 megabytes

There is a matrix A of size n x n where A_{i,j} = j for all 1 <= i, j <= n.
In one operation, you can select a row and reverse any subarray* in it.
Find a sequence of at most 2n operations such that every column will contain a permutation^dagger of length n.
It can be proven that the construction is always possible. If there are multiple solutions, output any of them.

    An array a is a subarray of an array b if a can be obtained from b by deleting zero or more elements from the beginning and zero or more elements from the end.
    ^dagger A permutation of length n is an array consisting of n distinct integers from 1 to n in arbitrary order. For example, [2, 3, 1, 5, 4] is a permutation, but [1, 2, 2] is not a permutation (2 appears twice in the array), and [1, 3, 4] is also not a permutation (n = 3 but there is 4 in the array).

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1 <= t <= 100). The description of the test cases follows.
The first line of each test case contains one integer n (3 <= n <= 5000) — denoting the number of rows and columns in the matrix.
It is guaranteed that the sum of n over all test cases does not exceed 5000.

Output:
For each test case, on the first line, print an integer k (0 <= k <= 2n), the number of operations you wish to perform. On the next lines, you should print the operations.
To print an operation, use the format "i l r" (1 <= l <= r <= n and 1 <= i <= n) which reverses the subarray A_{i,l}, A_{i,l+1}, ..., A_{i,r}.

Strategy:
We can choose a pivot point for each row. If we make the pivot point different for each row and rotate [0, pivot] and (pivot, n-1] we will get each number in each column once. This is because the pivot will basically split the row into two parts which form a sort of circular array and we will just be shifting it by 1 spot each row (only conceptually though, we are reversing 2 parts of the array to achieve the same effect in 2 reversals). This guarantees that after getting to the last row we will use up all of the numbers and since each row is just 1 to n the columns will each form a permutation.